From d13f274cfc42d5dd7915887bd46cbfa890be1be8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 01:49:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] bluez5: use audio.rate for the per-device rate, and list
 selectable rates

The per-device rate preference is the same concept the ALSA plugin
already exposes on its nodes as SPA_KEY_AUDIO_RATE ("audio.rate"), via
the same SPA_PROP_params mechanism (spa/plugins/alsa/alsa-pcm.c). Use
that spelling rather than inventing a bluez-specific one, so a client
that knows how to ask an audio object for a rate does not need to know
which plugin is behind it. bluez5.default.rate keeps its meaning as the
monitor-wide default that this overrides.

Also list the rates that can actually be selected, as the values of the
audio.rate property on the device PropInfo param. Without this a client
has to guess: the sink node advertises exactly one rate, because
EnumFormat is built by codec->enum_config() from the configuration that
was negotiated, not from what the codec and the remote could agree on.

The codec vtable has no rate table to query, so the rates are found by
probing select_config() with each candidate and collecting the distinct
results. That needs no per-codec knowledge and automatically reflects
what the remote endpoint offers, since select_config() already picks
from the intersection of the two.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
